From 5d46d219044f9f4509d5da99a6fecfbe0f501ce1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Samuel Price <thesamprice@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 03:06:38 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 2/5] microblaze: use lwr and swr for byte-reversed loads and
 stores

With the reorder instructions (-mxl-reorder, on by default for -mcpu=
later than v8.30.a, and at v8.30.a with -mxl-pattern-compare),
MicroBlaze has lwr and swr, which load and store a word with its bytes
reversed. The port's only pattern for them, movsi4_rev, takes the
bswap:SI of an SFmode operand, which no RTL ever has, so they were never
used: __builtin_bswap32 (*p) was lw and swapb, and *p =
__builtin_bswap32 (x) swapb and sw.

Replace movsi4_rev with *bswapsi2_load and *bswapsi2_store, which match
bswap:SI of a word in memory and a store of bswap:SI to memory, so that
combine merges the swap into the access.  lwr and swr exist only in the
register form, so a new predicate and memory constraint, Y, accept a
word addressed by a register or by the sum of two; any other address
keeps lwi and swapb, which is no longer than materializing the address
would be.  The halfword lhur and shr are left alone: they also reverse
the halfword's address within the word, so they are not a bswap:HI of
the access.

A loop converting 1024 words with d[i] = __builtin_bswap32 (s[i]) at
-O2 -mcpu=v10.0 goes from 6158 to 5134 instructions under QEMU, one
instruction in six per word, and summing byte-reversed words or storing
byte-reversed values likewise, from 6160 to 5136 and 6157 to 5134.

others/bswap-mem-run.c checks the loads and stores, with plain and
indexed addresses, against the bytes in memory, for either byte order.

Tested on microblaze-elf and microblazeel-elf under QEMU:
gcc.c-torture/execute.exp and gcc.target/microblaze, with no new
failures.

Assisted-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

